{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAptfrrqyDPW7RMaEPUL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Google is treating your note pages as \"not found\" because robots.txt blocks the API they load their content from. I fixed robots.txt (plus a smaller change to `site/app.js`) and wrote it all up in `ANSWER.md`.\n\n**Cause:** On 2026-09-07, `Disallow: /v1/` was added to robots.txt. Every page is the same HTML shell, and `app.js` loads the note from `GET /v1/notes/{slug}`. Google runs that JavaScript when it renders the page, and it obeys robots.txt for those requests, so the API call is never made. When it fails, the app shows \"NOT FOUND — No note lives at this address\", but the page still returns HTTP 200. That combination is what Google calls a Soft 404.\n\nThe URL Inspection export shows exactly this: the page fetch succeeded with HTTP 200, but the rendered text is the NOT FOUND screen. The dates match too: pages last crawled before Sep 7 are still indexed, and every one crawled since is a Soft 404. Browsers and curl ignore robots.txt, which is why the pages look fine to you.\n\n**Fix:**\n- **`site/robots.txt`:** added `Allow: /v1/notes` for the public read endpoints the pages need, and `Disallow: /v1/notes/*/reactions` to keep the write-only reactions route blocked. Google uses the longest matching rule, so these beat the general `Disallow: /v1/`, which stays. `/v1/me`, `/v1/me/drafts`, `/v1/auth/token` and `/admin/` are still blocked.\n- **`tools/check_robots.py`:** a small checker that applies robots rules the way Google does. All 12 test paths pass on the new file; the old file fails on exactly the two endpoints the pages need.\n- **`site/app.js`:** previously any failed load showed \"NOT FOUND\". Now only a real 404 from the API does, and it also marks the page `noindex`. Other failures show \"Couldn't load this note\". This is extra protection; the robots.txt change is the actual fix.\n\nI haven't deployed anything or touched Search Console. Once you deploy:\n1. Check the live robots.txt with curl.\n2. Request a robots.txt recrawl in Search Console.\n3. Run \"Test live URL\" on `/n/drip-line-pressure` and confirm it shows the note.\n4. Request indexing for the four Soft 404 pages.\n\n**Colleague's note:** the diagnosis and steps 1–5 and 7 matched your site almost exactly; only the paths differ, and your site has more per-user routes to keep blocked. I skipped step 6 (adding plain links to the HTML) because it wasn't the cause there or here, and this site has no build step to generate them. Their recovery timeline is about their site, not yours."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2221,"cache_read_input_tokens":26998,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2221},"output_tokens":761,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f48ba07-51c1-4c6e-b5b6-8e31a32a99bd","uuid":"ab17eb06-eb8d-44a6-b561-8f34b57b50be","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:43:24.932Z","request_id":"req_011CgAptf8xq6tuSWcVEbv1k"}
